IO-01: reject "nan" and "inf" as numeric input

getDouble() and the decimal-hours branch of datetime_strToTime() accept any
token that strtod() consumes, and C99 strtod() also reads "nan", "inf",
"infinity" and overflowing values such as 1e400. The range checks that follow
(x <= 0, x < 0) are false for NaN, so a NaN roughness, depth, series value or
routing step reached the simulation and gave NaN results, or a continuity
error printed as 0.000. Both functions now return 0 for a non-finite result,
so every caller reports ERROR 211 (or ERROR 213 for a date/time) for the
line, as for any other unreadable number. getInt() goes through getDouble()
and is covered too. Finite values are read exactly as before.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply IO-01_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/datetime.c b/src/legacy/engine/datetime.c
index 31eb5bc..8118bce 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/datetime.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/datetime.c
@@ -351,6 +351,7 @@ int datetime_strToTime(char* s, DateTime* t)
     *t = strtod(s, &endptr);
     if ( *endptr == 0 )
     {
+        if ( !isfinite(*t) ) return 0;     // strtod also reads "nan", "inf"
         *t /= 24.0;
         return 1;
     }
diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/input.c b/src/legacy/engine/input.c
index 5a58319..a8c13bf 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/input.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/input.c
@@ -859,6 +859,7 @@ int getDouble(char *s, double *y)
     char *endptr;
     *y = strtod(s, &endptr);
     if (*endptr > 0) return(0);
+    if (!isfinite(*y)) return(0);       // strtod also reads "nan" and "inf"
     return(1);
 }
 
